WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.084 --> 00:00:03.795
We work in a world of imagination.

00:00:04.087 --> 00:00:08.049
As humans, we're always projecting
 ourselves onto any characters that we see.

00:00:08.425 --> 00:00:11.219
We may see Lightning McQueen as a car,

00:00:11.219 --> 00:00:15.140
but we're still interpreting
 that as Owen Wilson as a white male.

00:00:15.223 --> 00:00:19.144
It's always really striking to me
 that we can imagine talking cars,

00:00:19.144 --> 00:00:21.771
but we can't imagine female NASCAR racers.

00:00:22.313 --> 00:00:24.524
It feels like we should be
 able to do this.

00:00:24.774 --> 00:00:26.735
We should be able to do this.

00:00:26.735 --> 00:00:30.363
But representation in the movie
 industry has been an ongoing fight.

00:00:30.697 --> 00:00:32.949
The world of animation is no different.

00:00:33.283 --> 00:00:37.287
According to a report by the USC Annenberg
 Inclusion Initiative,

00:00:37.454 --> 00:00:41.791
only 17% of 120 recent animated films

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:47.380
featured female characters as the lead
 or co-lead, and only 39% of 100 recent

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:50.884
animated TV series
 featured a largely female cast.

00:00:51.134 --> 00:00:54.429
Disney touts itself as a progressive
 and diverse company.

00:00:54.721 --> 00:00:58.266
Studies from their 2021 fiscal year
 show their ongoing strive

00:00:58.266 --> 00:01:00.185
for diversity in the workplace.

00:01:00.185 --> 00:01:03.646
But this specific problem is less
 about the people behind the screen

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:07.484
and more so about the characters
 and speaking lines in animation.

00:01:07.984 --> 00:01:12.363
My goal is to kind of normalize
 seeing diversity on screen.

00:01:12.489 --> 00:01:16.785
Being able to present
 that on a large scale is incredible.

00:01:21.915 --> 00:01:24.375
This is Jessica Heidt,
 the department manager

00:01:24.375 --> 00:01:28.379
of feature films at Pixar,
 before managing the art department.

00:01:28.546 --> 00:01:32.759
Her work in discoveries at Pixar changed
 the way in which scripts were digested.

00:01:33.051 --> 00:01:33.885
Several years ago.

00:01:33.885 --> 00:01:37.472
I started a program
 or a process of tracking gender

00:01:37.764 --> 00:01:40.600
across our characters
 and sort of trying to work with

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:43.353
increasing screen
 representation and diversity on screen.

00:01:44.104 --> 00:01:45.688
I got started in theater.

00:01:45.688 --> 00:01:46.523
I worked at a theater

00:01:46.523 --> 00:01:50.568
called the Magic Theater in San Francisco
 because we were a smaller theater,

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:54.239
we were able to be
 a lot more diverse in our choices.

00:01:54.864 --> 00:01:58.910
I know personally as a director,
 I was absolutely drawn to those stories.

00:01:58.910 --> 00:02:01.788
My transition to Pixar
 actually came directly from theater

00:02:01.788 --> 00:02:04.833
because Mary Coleman,
 who had been the associate artistic

00:02:04.833 --> 00:02:07.669
director at the Magic
 Theater, had transitioned over to Pixar.

00:02:07.877 --> 00:02:10.130
She would bring me in
 to do script coverage,

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:12.882
which was basically reading scripts
 and writing analysis.

00:02:13.133 --> 00:02:15.927
The continuous reading,
 writing and analyzing

00:02:15.927 --> 00:02:19.514
surprised Jessica,
 after she noticed one major commonality.

00:02:20.056 --> 00:02:21.850
I first noticed that
 there was a discrepancy

00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:24.727
between the male and female characters
 when I was working on Cars 3.

00:02:25.186 --> 00:02:26.604
What I was sort of feeling was that, "Oh,

00:02:26.604 --> 00:02:30.650
this feels very uneven", and so I went in
 and actually counted the difference.

00:02:30.733 --> 00:02:35.029
And at that point,
 it was 90% male and only 10% female.

00:02:35.446 --> 00:02:38.950
It was so stark it became something
 that I think I was unable

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:42.328
to step away from and say, "You
 know, this isn't a problem".

00:02:42.328 --> 00:02:45.039
I had gone back and tracked the data
 across all of our films.

00:02:45.123 --> 00:02:48.918
At that point where we were
 was about 75% male

00:02:48.918 --> 00:02:51.254
for speaking characters
 and for the line count.

00:02:51.254 --> 00:02:53.131
Take a look at Pixar's earlier movies

00:02:53.131 --> 00:02:55.884
and one of their most popular
 franchises, Toy Story.

00:02:56.134 --> 00:03:00.054
With the exception of Andy's mom, sister,
 and Bo Peep, virtually

00:03:00.054 --> 00:03:03.641
all of the characters
 and speaking lines are predominantly male.

00:03:04.601 --> 00:03:09.147
When I finally was
 able to present this to the executives,

00:03:09.397 --> 00:03:12.358
I did it with a collection
 of charts and graphs.

00:03:13.193 --> 00:03:15.904
Because for me, being able
 to take the emotion

00:03:16.279 --> 00:03:19.199
out of what we were doing
 and to just be able

00:03:19.199 --> 00:03:21.701
to have them look at the numbers
 and it was irrefutable.

00:03:22.202 --> 00:03:25.580
I remember being absolutely terrified
 in that meeting of just, you know,

00:03:26.122 --> 00:03:30.919
handing them out and wondering
 what people were going to say as this room

00:03:31.711 --> 00:03:35.298
of of all of the top
 execs were were in there looking at it.

00:03:35.548 --> 00:03:39.510
Not only were the executives open
 to hearing about Jessica's discoveries,

00:03:39.594 --> 00:03:40.637
but they immediately

00:03:40.637 --> 00:03:45.225
put a plan in action working together
 to come up with the solution. As a studio.

00:03:45.266 --> 00:03:49.562
We have the gift really, of working
 with these incredible technical minds

00:03:49.812 --> 00:03:51.981
as well as these storytellers.

00:03:51.981 --> 00:03:53.942
And so every time you come up

00:03:53.942 --> 00:03:57.153
with an idea of like, "Oh,
 here's something I would like to change".

00:03:57.820 --> 00:04:01.532
A technical wizard will come in
 and say, "Oh, I can create a tool

00:04:01.532 --> 00:04:04.744
for that to make it really easy
 and really scalable".

00:04:04.744 --> 00:04:07.914
I had some incredible allies, the biggest

00:04:07.914 --> 00:04:10.917
and most amazing one being Josh Miner,
 who is in our tools department.

00:04:11.251 --> 00:04:12.669
He was thinking about it from a technical

00:04:12.669 --> 00:04:14.963
point of view of like, "Is there
 something that we can be doing

00:04:15.004 --> 00:04:17.757
as an organization that might be able
 to address these problems?"

00:04:17.757 --> 00:04:22.345
And it was really Josh that said "Hey,
 we can use this tool that we already have

00:04:22.345 --> 00:04:25.807
in Pixar that we use
 as script supervisors to track data".

00:04:26.641 --> 00:04:30.728
Now this is something that the executives,
 the producers, the directors, anyone

00:04:30.728 --> 00:04:34.440
on any of the creatives on
 any of our movies are now expecting

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:38.152
this information and it becomes part
 of the conversation at every brain trust.

00:04:38.361 --> 00:04:43.491
In 2019, Pixar created SparkShorts,
 a series of short animated films

00:04:43.491 --> 00:04:47.453
designed to give women and people of color
 more opportunities in animation.

00:04:47.787 --> 00:04:52.292
There was really a space
 where the cultural moment outside of me

00:04:52.292 --> 00:04:57.630
outside of Pixar really shifted,
 and I feel like the momentum of

00:04:57.630 --> 00:05:01.426
that was really something
 that helped ricochet the work

00:05:01.426 --> 00:05:05.013
that we're doing into the sort of level
 that we're able to do right now.

00:05:05.305 --> 00:05:08.308
Black Lives Matter
 and #MeToo were those cultural moments

00:05:08.308 --> 00:05:12.186
that catapulted a shift towards producing
 more diverse and inclusive movies.

00:05:12.312 --> 00:05:16.399
Pixar films like Coco and Soul showed the level of success when diversity

00:05:16.399 --> 00:05:20.320
and representation are showcased,
 and look at the cast of Toy Story 4.

00:05:20.528 --> 00:05:23.448
Fast forward through the franchise,
 and we see the introduction

00:05:23.448 --> 00:05:25.450
and inclusion of characters like Mrs.

00:05:25.450 --> 00:05:28.578
Potato Head, Jesse,
 Dolly and characters like

00:05:28.578 --> 00:05:31.664
Bo Peep were given more meaningful
 roles in the main narrative.

00:05:32.957 --> 00:05:35.209
In animation, it
 takes so long to make our films,

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:39.047
so we are finally starting
 to see the receipts

00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:42.675
of the work that got started in 2016.

00:05:42.717 --> 00:05:47.013
You know, in 2017, when we really, as
 a studio, started paying close attention.

00:05:47.013 --> 00:05:48.556
As you see the next films

00:05:48.556 --> 00:05:52.060
coming out, you're going to be seeing
 a lot more diversity on the screen.

00:05:52.602 --> 00:05:56.439
I also think that we're starting
 to see more women, more people of color,

00:05:56.439 --> 00:06:00.109
more more people in the creative spaces
 that are creating.

00:06:00.109 --> 00:06:04.447
And I think that naturally leads
 to more diverse representation on screen.

00:06:05.114 --> 00:06:08.242
I know it's not always the big jumps
 that we that we want,

00:06:08.242 --> 00:06:09.369
but I feel like it's starting.

